---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import {FAQSection, RelatedArticles} from '../../components/article';

const title = 'Complete Home Office Checklist: Everything You Need';
const description = 'The ultimate home office checklist for remote workers. From essential furniture to tech equipment, lighting, and ergonomics—build your perfect workspace.';
const author = 'FloatJet Team';
const authorUrl = '/about';
const pubDate = new Date('2024-12-15');
const readingTime = 16;
const image = '/images/unsplash/photo-1518455027359-f3f8164ba6bd-1200w.webp';
const imageAlt = 'Well-organized home office setup with desk, chair, and equipment';

const headings = [
  {depth: 2, slug: 'introduction', text: 'Build Your Perfect Workspace'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'furniture-checklist', text: 'Essential Furniture Checklist'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'tech-equipment', text: 'Tech Equipment Checklist'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'lighting-checklist', text: 'Lighting Checklist'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'organization-storage', text: 'Organization and Storage'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'comfort-ergonomics', text: 'Comfort and Ergonomics'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'budget-tiers', text: 'Optional Upgrades by Budget Tier'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'printable-checklist', text: 'Printable Checklist Summary'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'faq', text: 'Frequently Asked Questions'}
];

const faqData = [
  {
    question: 'How much should I budget for a home office setup?',
    answer: 'A functional home office can be set up for $500-800 with budget options. A comfortable mid-range setup runs $1,500-2,500. Premium setups with high-end ergonomic furniture and equipment can cost $3,000-5,000 or more.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What\'s the most important item to invest in?',
    answer: 'Your chair. You\'ll spend 8+ hours a day sitting, and a poor chair leads to back pain and long-term health issues. Invest in a quality ergonomic chair first, even if you save money on other items.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I need a dedicated room for a home office?',
    answer: 'Not necessarily. Many people work successfully from a corner of a bedroom, living room, or even a large closet. The key is having a dedicated space you can set up for work and mentally "leave" at the end of the day.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I buy a standing desk?',
    answer: 'Standing desks offer health benefits by allowing you to alternate between sitting and standing. They\'re not essential, but if your budget allows, a sit-stand desk is a worthwhile investment for long-term health.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What monitor size is best for remote work?',
    answer: 'For most remote workers, a 27-inch monitor hits the sweet spot. It\'s large enough for comfortable viewing and multitasking without being overwhelming. Dual monitors or an ultrawide (34"+) are great if you work with multiple applications.'
  },
  {
    question: 'How important is lighting in a home office?',
    answer: 'Very important. Poor lighting causes eye strain, headaches, and fatigue. Ideally, combine natural light with a good desk lamp. Position your desk to avoid screen glare and ensure your face is well-lit for video calls.'
  }
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Ultimate Home Office Setup',
    excerpt: 'Complete guide to building your ideal remote workspace.',
    href: '/blog/ultimate-home-office-setup',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1518455027359-f3f8164ba6bd-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Modern home office setup'
  },
  {
    title: 'Best Standing Desks',
    excerpt: 'Top standing desks for health and productivity.',
    href: '/gear/best-standing-desks',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1593062096033-9a26b09da705-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Standing desk in home office'
  },
  {
    title: 'Best Ergonomic Chairs',
    excerpt: 'Find the perfect chair for long work sessions.',
    href: '/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1580480055273-228ff5388ef8-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Ergonomic office chair'
  }
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Setup"
    categorySlug="guides/setup"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Setting up a home office can feel overwhelming—there's furniture, tech, lighting, organization,
    and ergonomics to consider. This comprehensive checklist covers everything you need to create
    a productive, comfortable remote workspace.
  </p>

  <h2 id="introduction">Build Your Perfect Workspace</h2>

  <p>
    Whether you're setting up your first home office or upgrading an existing space, having a
    clear checklist ensures you don't miss anything important. A well-designed workspace boosts
    productivity, protects your health, and makes remote work more enjoyable.
  </p>

  <p>
    This guide breaks down everything into categories with essential items and optional upgrades.
    You don't need everything on day one—start with the essentials and add items as your budget allows.
  </p>

  <h2 id="furniture-checklist">Essential Furniture Checklist</h2>

  <h3>The Desk</h3>
  <p>Your desk is the foundation of your workspace. Consider:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> Minimum 48" wide for comfortable work; 60" if you use multiple monitors</li>
    <li><strong>Height:</strong> Standard desks are 28-30" tall; adjustable is ideal</li>
    <li><strong>Standing option:</strong> Sit-stand desks let you alternate positions throughout the day</li>
    <li><strong>Surface:</strong> Enough depth (24"+) for monitor at proper distance</li>
    <li><strong>Cable management:</strong> Built-in grommets or cable trays are a plus</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Chair</h3>
  <p>Don't skimp on your chair—it's your most important purchase:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Adjustable height:</strong> Feet flat on floor, thighs parallel to ground</li>
    <li><strong>Lumbar support:</strong> Adjustable lumbar support prevents lower back pain</li>
    <li><strong>Armrests:</strong> Adjustable armrests that support elbows at 90 degrees</li>
    <li><strong>Seat depth:</strong> 2-4 fingers of space between seat edge and back of knees</li>
    <li><strong>Breathable material:</strong> Mesh backs help with temperature regulation</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Additional Furniture</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Monitor stand or arm:</strong> Raises screen to eye level</li>
    <li><strong>Footrest:</strong> If your feet don't reach the floor comfortably</li>
    <li><strong>Filing cabinet or drawers:</strong> For document storage</li>
    <li><strong>Bookshelf:</strong> For reference materials and supplies</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tech-equipment">Tech Equipment Checklist</h2>

  <h3>Computing Essentials</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Computer:</strong> Laptop or desktop with specs matching your work requirements</li>
    <li><strong>External monitor:</strong> 24-27" minimum; larger or dual monitors for heavy multitasking</li>
    <li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> External keyboard if using laptop; ergonomic or mechanical for comfort</li>
    <li><strong>Mouse:</strong> Ergonomic mouse to prevent wrist strain</li>
    <li><strong>Mouse pad:</strong> Large pad with wrist rest for comfort</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Video Conferencing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Webcam:</strong> 1080p minimum; 4K for professional presentations</li>
    <li><strong>Microphone:</strong> USB mic or quality headset; laptop mics are usually poor</li>
    <li><strong>Headphones:</strong> Noise-canceling for focus and clear calls</li>
    <li><strong>Ring light or desk lamp:</strong> Front-facing light for video calls</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Connectivity</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>USB hub or dock:</strong> Expand laptop connectivity</li>
    <li><strong>Ethernet cable:</strong> Wired connection is more reliable than WiFi</li>
    <li><strong>Power strip with surge protection:</strong> Protect your equipment</li>
    <li><strong>UPS (battery backup):</strong> Prevents work loss during power outages</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="lighting-checklist">Lighting Checklist</h2>

  <h3>Natural Light</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Desk position:</strong> Perpendicular to windows to avoid glare</li>
    <li><strong>Window treatments:</strong> Blinds or curtains to control brightness</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid backlighting:</strong> Don't sit with bright window behind you for video calls</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Artificial Lighting</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Desk lamp:</strong> Adjustable LED lamp with multiple brightness levels</li>
    <li><strong>Ambient lighting:</strong> Overhead light or floor lamp to reduce contrast</li>
    <li><strong>Task lighting:</strong> Focused light for reading documents</li>
    <li><strong>Color temperature:</strong> 4000-5000K (neutral white) for work; warmer for evenings</li>
    <li><strong>Video call lighting:</strong> Front-facing light at face level</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="organization-storage">Organization and Storage</h2>

  <h3>Desk Organization</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pen/pencil holder:</strong> Keep writing tools accessible</li>
    <li><strong>Document tray:</strong> For incoming paperwork</li>
    <li><strong>Drawer organizers:</strong> Prevent junk drawer chaos</li>
    <li><strong>Monitor shelf:</strong> Creates storage under raised monitor</li>
    <li><strong>Desktop organizer:</strong> For small items, sticky notes, etc.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Cable Management</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cable clips:</strong> Keep cables routed neatly</li>
    <li><strong>Cable sleeve:</strong> Bundle multiple cables together</li>
    <li><strong>Under-desk tray:</strong> Hide power strips and excess cable</li>
    <li><strong>Velcro ties:</strong> Adjustable cable bundling</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Filing and Documents</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filing cabinet:</strong> For important documents</li>
    <li><strong>Scanner:</strong> Digitize documents to reduce paper clutter</li>
    <li><strong>Shredder:</strong> Securely dispose of sensitive documents</li>
    <li><strong>Label maker:</strong> Keep everything clearly labeled</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="comfort-ergonomics">Comfort and Ergonomics</h2>

  <h3>Monitor Positioning</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distance:</strong> Arm's length from your eyes (20-26 inches)</li>
    <li><strong>Height:</strong> Top of screen at or slightly below eye level</li>
    <li><strong>Tilt:</strong> Slight backward tilt to reduce glare</li>
    <li><strong>Multiple monitors:</strong> Primary monitor directly ahead; secondary at slight angle</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Keyboard and Mouse</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Keyboard height:</strong> Elbows at 90 degrees, wrists straight</li>
    <li><strong>Wrist rest:</strong> Optional; use during breaks, not while typing</li>
    <li><strong>Mouse position:</strong> Close to keyboard, same height</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Comfort Items</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Footrest:</strong> If chair doesn't allow feet flat on floor</li>
    <li><strong>Seat cushion:</strong> Additional comfort for long sessions</li>
    <li><strong>Back support pillow:</strong> If chair lacks adequate lumbar support</li>
    <li><strong>Anti-fatigue mat:</strong> For standing desk users</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Environment</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Temperature control:</strong> Small fan or space heater if needed</li>
    <li><strong>Plants:</strong> Improve air quality and reduce stress</li>
    <li><strong>White noise machine:</strong> Block distracting sounds</li>
    <li><strong>Air purifier:</strong> Especially important in small spaces</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="budget-tiers">Optional Upgrades by Budget Tier</h2>

  <h3>Budget Tier ($500-800)</h3>
  <p>Focus on essentials that directly impact your work and health:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Basic ergonomic chair ($150-250)</li>
    <li>Simple desk ($100-200)</li>
    <li>External monitor ($150-200)</li>
    <li>Keyboard and mouse ($50-100)</li>
    <li>Desk lamp ($30-50)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Mid-Range Tier ($1,500-2,500)</h3>
  <p>Better quality and more comfort features:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Quality ergonomic chair ($400-600)</li>
    <li>Electric standing desk ($400-600)</li>
    <li>27" 4K monitor ($300-400)</li>
    <li>Quality webcam ($100-150)</li>
    <li>Good headset ($100-200)</li>
    <li>Proper lighting setup ($100-150)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Premium Tier ($3,000-5,000+)</h3>
  <p>Best-in-class equipment for serious remote workers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Premium ergonomic chair—Herman Miller, Steelcase ($1,000-1,500)</li>
    <li>Motorized standing desk with memory presets ($700-1,000)</li>
    <li>Dual monitors or ultrawide ($600-1,000)</li>
    <li>Professional microphone setup ($200-400)</li>
    <li>4K webcam ($200-300)</li>
    <li>Mechanical keyboard ($150-300)</li>
    <li>Premium peripherals and accessories ($300-500)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="printable-checklist">Printable Checklist Summary</h2>

  <h3>Absolute Essentials</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>☐ Desk (appropriate size for your work)</li>
    <li>☐ Ergonomic chair</li>
    <li>☐ Computer (laptop or desktop)</li>
    <li>☐ External monitor</li>
    <li>☐ External keyboard</li>
    <li>☐ Mouse</li>
    <li>☐ Desk lamp</li>
    <li>☐ Headset or headphones</li>
    <li>☐ Webcam</li>
    <li>☐ Reliable internet connection</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Highly Recommended</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>☐ Monitor stand or arm</li>
    <li>☐ Cable management solution</li>
    <li>☐ Power strip with surge protection</li>
    <li>☐ USB hub or dock</li>
    <li>☐ Footrest (if needed)</li>
    <li>☐ Desk organizer</li>
    <li>☐ Good lighting for video calls</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Nice to Have</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>☐ Standing desk or desk converter</li>
    <li>☐ Noise-canceling headphones</li>
    <li>☐ Second monitor</li>
    <li>☐ UPS battery backup</li>
    <li>☐ Document scanner</li>
    <li>☐ Plants</li>
    <li>☐ White noise machine</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2>Start Building Your Workspace</h2>

  <p>
    You don't need to buy everything at once. Start with the absolute essentials—a decent chair,
    desk, and your computing equipment. Then gradually add items as you identify what would most
    improve your workday.
  </p>

  <p>
    Remember: the best home office is one you'll actually use comfortably for years. Invest in
    quality where it matters most (chair, monitor, keyboard), and save on items where budget
    options work just as well. Your future self will thank you for taking the time to set up
    your workspace properly.
  </p>

  <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
</ArticleLayout>
